   "source": [
    "## 10. Conclusions\n",
    "\n",
    "This analysis compared the historical performance and risk characteristics of five major assets between 2020 and 2025 and evaluated the effect of combining them in an equal-weight portfolio.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Main Findings\n",
    "\n",
    "- Bitcoin generated the highest historical return but also exhibited the greatest volatility and maximum drawdown.\n",
    "- SPY and QQQ were highly correlated, demonstrating that holding multiple equity ETFs does not necessarily provide substantial diversification.\n",
    "- Gold displayed relatively low correlations with the other assets and recorded the smallest maximum drawdown in the analysis.\n",
    "- The equal-weight portfolio generated an annualized return of **23.32%** with annualized volatility of **18.89%**.\n",
    "- Under the assumed 0% risk-free rate, the equal-weight portfolio achieved the highest Sharpe ratio at **1.23**.\n",
    "- Diversification improved historical risk-adjusted performance but did not eliminate significant downside risk.\n",
    "\n",
    "Overall, the analysis demonstrates why investment performance should be evaluated using multiple dimensions of risk and return rather than absolute returns alone.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Limitations\n",
    "\n",
    "- The analysis is based exclusively on historical data from **2020–2025** and does not predict future performance.\n",
    "- A **0% risk-free rate** was assumed when calculating Sharpe ratios.\n",
    "- The portfolio uses fixed equal weights and does not perform portfolio optimization.\n",
    "- Transaction costs, taxes, management fees, inflation, and trading constraints are excluded.\n",
    "- The analysis uses a limited five-asset universe and therefore does not represent the full range of available investments.\n",
    "- Results are sensitive to the selected time period and assets.\n",
    "\n",
    "This project is intended as a demonstration of Python-based financial data analysis and portfolio risk analysis, not as investment advice."
   ]
